Generate synthesizable SystemVerilog RTL for a parameterized UART controller (transmitter + receiver) following these exact instructions:

TX FSM states: Idle, Start, Data, Parity, Stop, Done.
RX FSM states: Idle, Start Detect, Data, Parity, Stop, Error/Done.
Features required: configurable data bits (7/8), parity (none/even/odd), stop bits (1/2), framing/parity/overrun error flags, busy/done/valid handshake signals.

Hardware/design constraints:
- Target FPGA: AMD/Xilinx Artix-7
- Tool: Vivado 2025.2
- Must be synthesizable SystemVerilog
- System clock: 100 MHz; default baud rate: 115200; all timing derived from parameters CLK_FREQ_HZ and BAUD_RATE (no hardcoded divider values)
- Must use synchronous reset (active-high, on clk posedge)
- Must NOT infer any latches (every case/if in combinational logic must have a default assignment)
- The asynchronous rx_serial_in input must pass through a 2-flip-flop synchronizer before use (add ASYNC_REG attribute to the synchronizer registers)
- Receiver must use 16x oversampling with midpoint sampling and must reject false start bits
- Use a single shared baud-rate generator/oversampling tick generator; do not duplicate counters between TX and RX unnecessarily
- Use $clog2 for all counter widths; avoid width mismatches and use explicit sizing on all constants
- Minimize LUT and flip-flop usage; avoid unnecessary registers
- Target maximum operating frequency: 100 MHz with no combinational path exceeding one LUT-level chain that would violate timing
- Clearly separate: state register, next-state logic, datapath registers (shift register, bit counter, baud counter), and output logic
- tx_serial_out, tx_busy, tx_done, rx_valid, and all error flags must be registered (glitch-free)
- rx_valid and tx_done must be single-cycle pulses

Provide the complete RTL code only, followed by a brief explanation of how the design handles clock-domain synchronization, baud timing accuracy, and timing closure at 100 MHz.
